00:08:07.790 --> 00:08:12.040
And ensure that we have
a secure environment.

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:13.510
OK, so about security.

00:08:13.510 --> 00:08:16.183
Security is all about
protecting our customer data.

00:08:16.183 --> 00:08:17.850
There are lots of
regulations out there.

00:08:17.850 --> 00:08:21.400
They care about us trying
to protect customer data.

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:24.190
And we have to have
multiple layers of defense.

00:08:24.190 --> 00:08:28.330
You don't want to have a
single misconfiguration create

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:29.130
disaster.

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:32.419
So you have to put several
different things in place.

00:08:32.419 --> 00:08:34.780
Obviously you have to
have logging, monitoring,

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:37.700
compliance, audit, all
those kinds of things.

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:40.270
There are HIPAA
requirements that you

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:43.480
have to maintain certain
audit logs for seven years.

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:45.910
Some other data has to be
maintained for 40 or 50

00:08:45.910 --> 00:08:47.480
years, that kind of thing.

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:51.730
So you have to think about all
these kinds of considerations.

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:55.390
And we use several
products in our ecosystem

00:08:55.390 --> 00:09:00.460
to try and provide that
multiple layers of defense.

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:02.770
But by the same token, we
want to go to Cloud native

00:09:02.770 --> 00:09:04.870
as much as we possibly
can so we can test

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:06.810
new and different products.

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:09.040
We sort of heard about Anthos.

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:11.140
We've also heard this
just this morning

00:09:11.140 --> 00:09:14.140
about new artificial
intelligence,

00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:16.070
BI kinds of capabilities.

00:09:16.070 --> 00:09:19.370
So we want to take advantage
of those very, very quickly.

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:22.390
So we really like the
pay-as-you-go model.

00:09:22.390 --> 00:09:24.260
One of my executives--

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:25.960
I have it on the
side-- he talks about

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:29.930
as being a disposable
technology stack.

00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:33.077
But pay-as-you-go
means zero commitment.

00:09:33.077 --> 00:09:35.410
Traditionally, if you're going
to try and get something,

00:09:35.410 --> 00:09:37.870
you had to go in this year-long
or three-year-long kind

00:09:37.870 --> 00:09:39.490
of contract cycle.

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:42.310
In this world, I can
use it for three months

00:09:42.310 --> 00:09:45.920
and then deploy something
different if I need to.

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:47.470
Now traditional
technologies really

00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:48.850
haven't caught up with this.

00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:51.430
This is a real challenge
for a lot of the vendors.

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:54.700
Some of the vendors that you
saw on Vivian's earlier slide,

00:09:54.700 --> 00:09:56.500
they still sort of
try and move you

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:58.690
toward a one or a
three or a five year

00:09:58.690 --> 00:10:01.720
commit on purchasing
some of the licenses.

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:06.280
So we as an industry need to
help vendors move into this

00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:07.580
pay-as-you-go model.

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:10.330
And it's going to be a
challenge for us all.

00:10:10.330 --> 00:10:12.680
When you start talking about
infrastructure as code,

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:14.890
everybody needs to
become a programmer.

00:10:14.890 --> 00:10:18.790
What I mean by that is rather
than having a Windows systems

00:10:18.790 --> 00:10:22.330
administrator try and
work with the windows GUI,

00:10:22.330 --> 00:10:24.040
everything needs to be in code.

00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:25.480
Everything needs to be in Git.

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:28.840
You push it up, the whole
instance is fully configured.

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:29.740
We do that for Linux.

00:10:29.740 --> 00:10:34.010
We do it for Windows
and so forth.

00:10:34.010 --> 00:10:36.590
So, again, protecting
our customers,

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:37.870
we use standard approaches.

00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:41.290
Because we're lifting and
shifting a lot of our workloads

00:10:41.290 --> 00:10:44.870
from on-premise data
centers into the Cloud.

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:48.470
And when you do that, you can't
change everything all at once.

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:50.770
So therefore, there are
tools and techniques

00:10:50.770 --> 00:10:54.190
have been used traditionally
in some of those security

00:10:54.190 --> 00:10:57.190
appliances that we
just can't throw away,

00:10:57.190 --> 00:10:58.630
that we have to maintain.

00:10:58.630 --> 00:11:01.070
And there is not a
Cloud native equivalent.

00:11:01.070 --> 00:11:05.620
So we had to bring those
appliances forward with us.

00:11:05.620 --> 00:11:07.765
And they're used largely
for the segmentation.

00:11:07.765 --> 00:11:09.640
There is some business
function put in there,

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:12.370
but it's largely for
segmentation and isolation

00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:14.560
of workloads.

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:17.925
There is also the challenge
of separation of duties.

00:11:17.925 --> 00:11:20.050
You hear a separation of
duties and quite often you

00:11:20.050 --> 00:11:23.290
think about two different
groups doing things, right?

00:11:23.290 --> 00:11:25.360
This group has to
do firewall rules.

00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:26.750
This group has to do this.

00:11:26.750 --> 00:11:29.342
This is a test team
and that kind of thing.

00:11:29.342 --> 00:11:31.300
Well, when you think
about DevOps and everybody

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:33.990
being a programmer and
click a button and now

00:11:33.990 --> 00:11:36.310
I have a full
production environment.

00:11:36.310 --> 00:11:39.220
That really challenges
that separation of duties.

00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:41.820
So therefore you have
to put into place CI

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:45.910
to CD workflows and checkpoints,
approval checkpoints,

00:11:45.910 --> 00:11:49.600
to make sure somebody doesn't
release something incorrectly--

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:53.730
either maliciously
or accidentally.

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:56.900
We also developed
standard patterns.

00:11:56.900 --> 00:11:57.500
We vet that.

00:11:57.500 --> 00:11:58.430
We think about it.

00:11:58.430 --> 00:11:59.540
We design it.

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:00.530
We test it.

00:12:00.530 --> 00:12:02.460
We develop standard
patterns and we

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:06.120
want to deploy those
on a consistent basis.

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:08.530
So we use something
called golden images.

00:12:08.530 --> 00:12:10.030
You could call it
whatever you want.

00:12:10.030 --> 00:12:13.910
It's sort of like a CD that
you can use that has everything

00:12:13.910 --> 00:12:16.760
on it that you would need.

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:20.330
That also helps prevent
the common mistakes

00:12:20.330 --> 00:12:21.980
that you're going
to naturally have.

00:12:21.980 --> 00:12:26.000
We also put that in
the network as well.

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:30.840
To ensure that we don't have an
accidental thing get through--

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:32.630
obviously that would
be a bad thing--

00:12:32.630 --> 00:12:35.660
we have reactive
controls as well.

00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:37.870
I'll give you a case in point.

00:12:37.870 --> 00:12:41.390
In the network, if someone puts
in a network firewall rule,

00:12:41.390 --> 00:12:42.780
that's incorrect.

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:45.470
We have Cloud functions that
automatically shut those down

00:12:45.470 --> 00:12:47.540
within seconds.

00:12:47.540 --> 00:12:50.470
We do the same thing in
some of our images as well.

00:12:53.070 --> 00:12:55.640
OK, so the golden images.

00:12:55.640 --> 00:12:57.320
Let me talk about that one.

00:12:57.320 --> 00:12:59.450
We take the Marketplace images.

00:12:59.450 --> 00:13:01.760
And we have a number of
them that we support.

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:04.880
We take those Marketplace
images and we use Packer.

00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:07.610
And we add on to that
marketplace image

00:13:07.610 --> 00:13:09.310
a number of different
security products

00:13:09.310 --> 00:13:11.480
and a number of different
configuration items

00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:16.100
that are necessary to run a VM
instance in the environment.

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:19.790
We take those and we have them
as a private image family.

00:13:19.790 --> 00:13:23.420
And we share that, authorize
use, give permissions

00:13:23.420 --> 00:13:26.790
to service projects,
authorize that use

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:27.950
to the service projects.

00:13:27.950 --> 00:13:31.250
So developers can just
pick those off the shelf.

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:35.610
And they have everything
that they need to be secure.

00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:39.890
So, again, we apply hardening,
CIS hardening scripts.

00:13:39.890 --> 00:13:42.230
We install binaries
and other products

00:13:42.230 --> 00:13:45.320
that we need to have on there
that are required for security

00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:47.240
compliance purposes.

00:13:47.240 --> 00:13:49.790
In Windows, we
CIS-prep the images.

00:13:49.790 --> 00:13:53.970
Or in Linux, we redact out
some configuration items.

00:13:53.970 --> 00:13:55.850
And then we do
automated pen testing.

00:13:55.850 --> 00:13:59.120
We actually deploy some of these
and do pen testing afterwards

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:01.280
to make sure it's all solid.

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:03.350
We try and do this
on a monthly basis.

00:14:03.350 --> 00:14:05.150
It's pretty much automated.

00:14:05.150 --> 00:14:08.540
But with things changing, you
know, some technologies change,

00:14:08.540 --> 00:14:10.500
there's some manual
in there as well.

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:12.230
But generally, it's
fairly well automated

00:14:12.230 --> 00:14:17.120
that when a new image
comes out, we update it.

00:14:17.120 --> 00:14:21.020
The other advantage about this
is once we put that up there,

00:14:21.020 --> 00:14:23.990
the boot image, so that
licensing material that

00:14:23.990 --> 00:14:26.700
needs to be there, that's
maintained with these images.

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:30.800
So there's no additional effort
on the developer's standpoint

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:34.320
to deploy something.

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:38.550
Now for special use cases, we
have some security appliances

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:40.740
which are really
special use cases.

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:42.270
They had many network adapters.

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:46.140
They had many,
many considerations

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:48.330
that you have to
take into account.

00:14:48.330 --> 00:14:50.250
We took the golden image--

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:52.710
took the image off
the marketplace

00:14:52.710 --> 00:14:54.790
that's provided by the vendor.

00:14:54.790 --> 00:14:57.420
And we have a batch script,
fairly simple batch script

00:14:57.420 --> 00:14:59.220
that runs Deployment Manager.

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:01.830
And the real physics
is in that YAML file.

00:15:01.830 --> 00:15:04.710
In the YAML file, we
put in all the material

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:07.410
that we need to
customize that image.

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:10.080
I use the term boiler
plate settings.

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:11.940
But it's all the things
that you need to do.

00:15:11.940 --> 00:15:16.560
For example, something silly,
like on every system image,

00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:19.650
you have to have the
disclaimer that your actions

00:15:19.650 --> 00:15:21.030
can be monitored, right?

00:15:21.030 --> 00:15:22.650
That's a legal requirement.

00:15:22.650 --> 00:15:24.430
Everybody has that
on their systems.

00:15:24.430 --> 00:15:27.150
So that has to be on
every single image.

00:15:27.150 --> 00:15:29.140
Where are the DNS servers?

00:15:29.140 --> 00:15:30.810
How are you doing NTP.

00:15:30.810 --> 00:15:33.570
All of that kind of stuff
is part of this boilerplate.

00:15:33.570 --> 00:15:36.150
Every image should get
this kind of material.

00:15:36.150 --> 00:15:38.820
So we put that all
in this YAML file.

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:43.710
And so from 0 to
60 in 11 minutes--

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:45.510
and that's with three
reboots because I'm

00:15:45.510 --> 00:15:48.180
switching network adapters
and provisioning modules

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:51.000
and resizing disk drives
and that kind of stuff.

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:54.900
Within 11 minutes, you can have
a fully production security

00:15:54.900 --> 00:16:02.170
appliance deployed and ready to
go, from 0 to 60 in 11 minutes.

00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:03.470
That's pretty powerful.

00:16:03.470 --> 00:16:05.940
Give you an example.

00:16:05.940 --> 00:16:09.355
Just last week, we had a
brand new engineer come on,

00:16:09.355 --> 00:16:11.480
very skilled in networking,
very skilled in working

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:13.670
with this class of appliance.

00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:16.730
And she had never
seen Git before.

00:16:16.730 --> 00:16:20.170
So I sat with her for an hour
and a half, going over Git,

00:16:20.170 --> 00:16:22.670
helping her to
understand Git and Bash

00:16:22.670 --> 00:16:24.480
and that kind of thing.

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:26.030
But once we went
through that, she

00:16:26.030 --> 00:16:28.490
was able to actually
use the deployment

00:16:28.490 --> 00:16:32.220
and build two instances
in 11 minutes.

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:33.650
So it was pretty
straightforward.

00:16:33.650 --> 00:16:37.910
Once I got her past all the,
"you're now a programmer,"

00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:42.000
she was able to do her job
very, very effectively.

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:44.750
So, therefore, she didn't
have to go and figure out

00:16:44.750 --> 00:16:47.000
all this YAML boilerplate,
and everything else.

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:48.890
Traditionally, when you
think about deploying

00:16:48.890 --> 00:16:51.050
some of these
hardware appliances,

00:16:51.050 --> 00:16:53.960
it's several hours of somebody
going through and configuring

00:16:53.960 --> 00:16:56.880
this and configuring that
and so forth and so on.

00:16:56.880 --> 00:17:02.040
So infrastructure as code
really pays the value.

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:05.244
So another one that
we were worried about

00:17:05.244 --> 00:17:06.869
is once you put one
of these appliances

00:17:06.869 --> 00:17:08.869
out there, what happens
if you have a disk drive

00:17:08.869 --> 00:17:10.589
failure, right?

00:17:10.589 --> 00:17:11.790
That's always a worry.

00:17:11.790 --> 00:17:14.069
And in the physical world,
you have two disk drives.

00:17:14.069 --> 00:17:18.230
And you have the active standby
pair and those kinds of things.

00:17:18.230 --> 00:17:20.730
Well, on the Cloud, you don't
have this active standby pair.

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:22.170
Everything's active active.

00:17:22.170 --> 00:17:24.930
OK, so what happens if
you lose a disk drive?

00:17:24.930 --> 00:17:26.069
Well, we take snapshots.

00:17:26.069 --> 00:17:28.140
We're doing hourly snapshots.

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:30.670
So the disk drive
gets corrupted.

00:17:30.670 --> 00:17:33.630
Which hour do you want
me to recreate from?

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:36.270
With the YAML file, deployment
manager, we can destroy,

00:17:36.270 --> 00:17:39.900
rebuild inside of five minutes.

00:17:39.900 --> 00:17:42.660
So if today right this second
somebody called me and said,

00:17:42.660 --> 00:17:47.400
I need to rebuild from seven
hours ago, I could take it,

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:49.432
five minutes, you've
got it back again.

00:17:49.432 --> 00:17:50.390
That's pretty powerful.

00:17:53.260 --> 00:17:55.810
On each one of our instances,
we have all the logging

00:17:55.810 --> 00:17:58.000
and monitoring tools.

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:01.890
This little icon there
is representative of one

00:18:01.890 --> 00:18:06.010
of the very common large scale
logging and monitoring tools

00:18:06.010 --> 00:18:07.640
that many people use.

00:18:07.640 --> 00:18:08.860
But we also use Stackdriver.

00:18:08.860 --> 00:18:12.280
We also use Cloud storage
as logging and monitoring.

00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:14.500
For example, I mentioned
that in some cases

00:18:14.500 --> 00:18:18.040
we have retention periods
of, you know, many years--

00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:21.130
7, 40, 50 years of retention.

00:18:21.130 --> 00:18:23.395
Doesn't make sense to maintain
a brown spinning disk.

00:18:26.710 --> 00:18:27.905
Sorry.

00:18:27.905 --> 00:18:30.110
It makes sense put
in Cloud storage

00:18:30.110 --> 00:18:35.330
so that you can have that
really long retention.

00:18:35.330 --> 00:18:37.400
Now, of course, you're
going to apply life cycles

00:18:37.400 --> 00:18:40.220
to all of that so that you're
going to throw away data

00:18:40.220 --> 00:18:43.500
that you don't need as
quickly as you need to.

00:18:43.500 --> 00:18:47.390
OK, so the buying models.

00:18:47.390 --> 00:18:49.700
How many people have ever
tried to-- you know, you

00:18:49.700 --> 00:18:51.200
get something and
they say, we'll

00:18:51.200 --> 00:18:53.240
give you a 30-day
free trial, you know,

00:18:53.240 --> 00:18:54.740
and so forth and so on.

00:18:54.740 --> 00:18:56.990
I don't know about you, but
I've never ever, not once,

00:18:56.990 --> 00:18:59.360
ever had a 30-day
free trial not get

00:18:59.360 --> 00:19:02.400
extended two or three times.

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:04.070
So, typically, what
you need is you

00:19:04.070 --> 00:19:09.650
need five hours of dedicated
time over 120 day period,

00:19:09.650 --> 00:19:11.195
right?

00:19:11.195 --> 00:19:12.820
I don't know about
you, but I get busy.

00:19:12.820 --> 00:19:15.160
And I run from meeting
to meeting to meeting.

00:19:15.160 --> 00:19:17.242
Well, in this case
what I can actually do

00:19:17.242 --> 00:19:19.450
is I can spin up an instance,
work on it for an hour,

00:19:19.450 --> 00:19:22.870
shut it down, go to meetings, go
off to a conference for a week,

00:19:22.870 --> 00:19:26.150
come back, run it for another
hour, and that kind of thing.

00:19:26.150 --> 00:19:29.390
So I have it over
a long duration.

00:19:29.390 --> 00:19:32.970
Again, we were talking about
this disposable technology.

00:19:32.970 --> 00:19:36.337
So one of the things since we
started adopting the Cloud--

00:19:36.337 --> 00:19:38.420
we've been on this journey
for several years now--

00:19:38.420 --> 00:19:41.780
is we did not want to
get into a position

00:19:41.780 --> 00:19:45.500
where I make a
technology decision today

00:19:45.500 --> 00:19:47.420
and I continue with
that technology

00:19:47.420 --> 00:19:49.760
just because I bought it.

00:19:49.760 --> 00:19:53.000
One of the challenges you run
into is as soon as you buy it,

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:55.040
then you wind up using
more of it, which

00:19:55.040 --> 00:19:56.855
means you are locked into it.

00:19:56.855 --> 00:19:58.730
And we want to be able
get out of that vendor

00:19:58.730 --> 00:20:00.960
lock in kind of case.

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:02.750
So when we start
thinking about some

00:20:02.750 --> 00:20:05.450
of these security
appliances, when I start,

00:20:05.450 --> 00:20:08.430
I said, great, that's my
current architecture for today.

00:20:08.430 --> 00:20:10.985
And if Google comes out with
a competitive advantage,

00:20:10.985 --> 00:20:14.040
I'm going to drop that one,
flip over to this other one.

00:20:14.040 --> 00:20:19.100
And so I don't want to be stuck
into a particular technology.

00:20:19.100 --> 00:20:22.030
Now that actually challenges--
if you think about it--

00:20:22.030 --> 00:20:25.190
the traditional purchasing
model, all right?

00:20:25.190 --> 00:20:31.330
So, in our case, we're a
publicly-traded company.

00:20:31.330 --> 00:20:34.630
And certain layers of management
have certain spend authorities.

00:20:34.630 --> 00:20:37.350
I'm sure you probably have
the same kind of thing.

00:20:37.350 --> 00:20:40.480
And in the old capex
model, it was, well,

00:20:40.480 --> 00:20:43.335
you have to do a capital
purchase request.

00:20:43.335 --> 00:20:44.710
It has to go
through procurement.

00:20:44.710 --> 00:20:48.190
It has to get 12 signatures
and so forth and so on.

00:20:48.190 --> 00:20:50.770
Some of these appliances
can be fairly expensive.

00:20:50.770 --> 00:20:54.850
One of them, as I've
shown here, is $85 a day.

00:20:54.850 --> 00:20:56.830
Well, after, you
know, I spin out

00:20:56.830 --> 00:20:59.870
five or six of these things,
run it for several days,

00:20:59.870 --> 00:21:03.010
if my manager doesn't have
the proper spend authority,

00:21:03.010 --> 00:21:04.820
now it can be in a problem.

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:08.080
So you constantly have to go
through education of people

00:21:08.080 --> 00:21:11.770
to make sure they understand
what you're spending.

00:21:11.770 --> 00:21:13.840
And turn off the lights.

00:21:13.840 --> 00:21:15.968
I talk about this all
the time to the people.

00:21:15.968 --> 00:21:18.010
Didn't your mom and dad
ever tell you to turn off

00:21:18.010 --> 00:21:19.580
the lights when
you leave the room?

00:21:19.580 --> 00:21:21.997
We need to always turn off the
lights whenever we possibly

00:21:21.997 --> 00:21:22.570
can.

00:21:22.570 --> 00:21:23.650
We do that across the board.

00:21:23.650 --> 00:21:25.025
We have a number
of tools that we

00:21:25.025 --> 00:21:28.720
use to try and turn off the
lights automatically based

00:21:28.720 --> 00:21:31.780
on time and also
based on utilization.

00:21:31.780 --> 00:21:36.260
But the other notion is build
it only when you need it.

00:21:36.260 --> 00:21:38.290
Don't build it two
weeks in advance.

00:21:38.290 --> 00:21:42.220
Build it the day or the hour
or the minute that you need it.

00:21:42.220 --> 00:21:45.090
And then destroy it as
quickly as you possibly can.

00:21:45.090 --> 00:21:46.930
That's a really
different mindset.

00:21:46.930 --> 00:21:50.170
If you ever worked
with any project team--

00:21:50.170 --> 00:21:53.187
for example, I was working with
one for our e-commerce team.

00:21:53.187 --> 00:21:55.770
And they were saying, we have
to have all these servers built.

00:21:55.770 --> 00:21:57.645
You know, we have to
have 10 servers this way

00:21:57.645 --> 00:21:58.790
and so forth and so on.

00:21:58.790 --> 00:22:01.660
And I said, well, OK,
when do you need them?

00:22:01.660 --> 00:22:02.390
On Friday.

00:22:02.390 --> 00:22:04.420
And I said, OK,
what time on Friday?

00:22:04.420 --> 00:22:06.190
They said by Friday afternoon.

00:22:06.190 --> 00:22:07.960
OK, I'll be build
them at lunchtime.

00:22:07.960 --> 00:22:09.880
No, no, no, we need
them by Monday.

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:11.020
Why?

00:22:11.020 --> 00:22:12.290
Because that's what we do.

00:22:12.290 --> 00:22:13.540
We have to get them on Monday.

00:22:13.540 --> 00:22:14.410
That's the project.

00:22:14.410 --> 00:22:16.687
You get them on Monday and
we'll use them on Friday.

00:22:16.687 --> 00:22:18.520
I said, no, I'm going
to do it at lunchtime.

00:22:18.520 --> 00:22:22.960
So, again, that model is
very different from most

00:22:22.960 --> 00:22:25.860
enterprises.

00:22:25.860 --> 00:22:31.080
So my summary slide,
the advice for others,

00:22:31.080 --> 00:22:34.470
I would recommend the
concept of the golden images.

00:22:34.470 --> 00:22:36.150
Now, there are exceptions.

00:22:36.150 --> 00:22:38.250
For example, I was
just mentioning

00:22:38.250 --> 00:22:43.440
there's a lot of chatter this
morning on an email back home.

00:22:43.440 --> 00:22:46.085
Somebody wants to use an image
off the straight marketplace.

00:22:46.085 --> 00:22:47.910
And I said, great,
you want to use

00:22:47.910 --> 00:22:49.380
the image off the marketplace?

00:22:49.380 --> 00:22:52.050
Then you get to go through
the five pages of steps

00:22:52.050 --> 00:22:54.270
that we go through to
build the golden image.

00:22:54.270 --> 00:22:55.570
But I don't want to do that.

00:22:55.570 --> 00:22:57.660
Well, then your choice
is to use a golden image.

00:22:57.660 --> 00:23:00.000
So we're calling
these not standards,

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:02.520
we call these
accelerators, trying

00:23:02.520 --> 00:23:04.140
to make it a
positive experience.

00:23:04.140 --> 00:23:06.360
If you use the
accelerator, you go fast.

00:23:06.360 --> 00:23:09.660
If you deviate from that
and use something different,

00:23:09.660 --> 00:23:12.800
it's going to slow you down.

00:23:12.800 --> 00:23:14.525
Have a solid maintenance
pattern of how

00:23:14.525 --> 00:23:16.400
you're going to actually
deploy these things,

00:23:16.400 --> 00:23:18.025
how are you actually
going to use them,

00:23:18.025 --> 00:23:21.090
and communicate that
with your customers.

00:23:21.090 --> 00:23:23.420
We use Shared Templates
for common deployments.

00:23:23.420 --> 00:23:26.800
In Deployment Manager, we
have a standard template.

00:23:26.800 --> 00:23:30.860
And in literally in
about 12 lines of YAML,

00:23:30.860 --> 00:23:33.050
the host name and whatever
else you're going--

00:23:33.050 --> 00:23:35.000
what network you
want to put it on.

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:37.070
In about 12 lines
of YAML, that's

00:23:37.070 --> 00:23:38.880
all you need to
spin up an instance.

00:23:38.880 --> 00:23:41.150
And you can get it in
two or three minutes.

00:23:41.150 --> 00:23:44.900
Domain joined if it's Windows
and all that kind of stuff.

00:23:44.900 --> 00:23:46.820
OK, use the power of
the startup script.

00:23:46.820 --> 00:23:48.990
That's how we're
doing some of that.

00:23:48.990 --> 00:23:51.830
And we also use Cloud
storage very heavily.

00:23:51.830 --> 00:23:54.650
For example, another
one is somebody

00:23:54.650 --> 00:23:57.280
wanted to create their
own Jupyter notebook.

00:23:57.280 --> 00:24:00.870
OK, so they wanted to create
a set of images for that.

00:24:00.870 --> 00:24:03.570
So what we did was we
use a golden image.

00:24:03.570 --> 00:24:08.440
And to do the Jupyter notebook,
it was a 61-gigabyte file

00:24:08.440 --> 00:24:10.380
that they needed to install.

00:24:10.380 --> 00:24:11.750
So we put that in Cloud storage.

00:24:11.750 --> 00:24:14.480
When the instant boots
up, it downloads it

00:24:14.480 --> 00:24:19.980
from Cloud storage,
configures it, there you go.

00:24:19.980 --> 00:24:21.450
And the last note
that I might say

00:24:21.450 --> 00:24:23.580
is if your folks
are not programmers,

00:24:23.580 --> 00:24:25.050
they need to become so.

00:24:25.050 --> 00:24:27.390
It's going to be a
challenge to not have

00:24:27.390 --> 00:24:29.680
programmers in your enterprise.

00:24:29.680 --> 00:24:31.620
So that means a skills
uplift at times.

00:24:31.620 --> 00:24:32.560
It means training.

00:24:32.560 --> 00:24:34.710
It means patience
with people learning

00:24:34.710 --> 00:24:39.250
how to use tools like Git and
manage infrastructure as code.

00:24:39.250 --> 00:24:41.985
So we'll have some time to
take questions later on.

00:24:41.985 --> 00:24:43.860
And I'm going to turn
it back over to Vivian.

00:24:47.580 --> 00:24:49.440
Sorry.

00:24:49.440 --> 00:24:51.000
VIVIAN XU: Thank you so much.

00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:55.860
It's always exciting to see
how GCP empowers enterprise

00:24:55.860 --> 00:24:59.800
and learn from the best
practice from those enterprise.

00:24:59.800 --> 00:25:04.440
So Ed mentioned, you know,
they start their deployment

00:25:04.440 --> 00:25:06.010
from GCP marketplace.

00:25:06.010 --> 00:25:08.730
So I want to just deep
dive a little bit more

00:25:08.730 --> 00:25:13.350
to the GCP marketplace and
also share other capabilities

00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:15.090
on the GCP marketplace with you.

00:25:17.640 --> 00:25:20.100
So we build the GCP
marketplace really

00:25:20.100 --> 00:25:22.830
for enterprise development.

00:25:22.830 --> 00:25:27.060
We enable the developers'
productivity, right?

00:25:27.060 --> 00:25:31.680
We have those vetted, tested
solutions on GCP marketplace.

00:25:31.680 --> 00:25:34.210
They can download and
use it right away.

00:25:34.210 --> 00:25:36.090
We have to simplify
the management.

00:25:36.090 --> 00:25:38.130
So we will talk about
the Deployment Manager.

00:25:38.130 --> 00:25:40.350
Ed also mentioned that's
something they use

00:25:40.350 --> 00:25:45.300
as well as some new capability
